What's in it:[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mCargo.toml[0m — [38;5;10mcrate-type = ["cdylib"][0m, [38;5;10mlib.name = "String_Slice"[0m so the artifact is [38;5;10mlibString_Slice.so[0m, matching the CMake target.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m — one exported function, [38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern "C" fn slice(*mut c_char, *mut c_int, *mut c_int) -> c_int[0m. The header has no namespace macros, so the linker symbol is plain [38;5;10mslice[0m (confirmed with [38;5;10mnm[0m).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two C behaviors worth calling out, both preserved rather than fixed:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mstart > len[0m and [38;5;10mstop > len[0m compare an [38;5;10mint[0m against a [38;5;10msize_t[0m, so the int is promoted to unsigned. A negative index becomes a huge value and trips the "off the end of the string" error instead of being caught as negative. Reproduced with [38;5;10m(start as usize) > len[0m.[0m[0m
- Output goes through the C runtime's [38;5;10mprintf[0m via an [38;5;10munsafe extern[0m declaration, not Rust's [38;5;10mprintln![0m. That keeps the [38;5;10m%.*s[0m precision semantics exact and, more importantly, shares stdio buffering with a C caller — Rust's stdout is always line-buffered while C's is block-buffered when redirected, which would reorder interleaved output in a redirected run.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Verification: built with [38;5;10mcargo build --release[0m (clean, no warnings), then linked the same C test harness against both the original C source and the Rust [38;5;10m.so[0m and diffed stdout over 45 cases — normal slices, empty string, boundary indices at [38;5;10mlen[0m, negative start/stop, [38;5;10mstop <= start[0m, and all four NULL-pointer combinations. Output was identical (1584 bytes, including 17 error-path lines). I did not test a NULL [38;5;10mmystr[0m, since the C dereferences it unconditionally via [38;5;10mstrlen[0m and would crash; the Rust does the same.[0m[0m
